# {{ if .DisplayName }}{{ .DisplayName }}{{ else }}{{ .Container }}{{ end }}

The `{{ .Container }}` scheduled job of {{ .SoftwareSystem }}, in project {{ .Project }}:
an EventBridge Scheduler schedule that runs an AWS Lambda function in
TypeScript, keeping a record of each run in a DynamoDB table, with its
infrastructure as a CDK app. Scaffolded by C4 Factory.

## The schedule

The job runs every day at 03:00 UTC: `SCHEDULE` in `infra/job_stack.ts`
says when, as a `cron` or `rate` expression of EventBridge Scheduler. Each
run gets the time it was scheduled for and which delivery it is:

```json
{ "scheduledTime": "2026-10-16T03:00:00Z", "attemptNumber": "1" }
```

The scheduled time is the run's key. The schedule and Lambda deliver a run
at least once; a run delivered again with the same scheduled time does
nothing twice. Here a run keeps a record of itself under that key: put the
job's own work in `runJob` in `src/job.ts`.

The schedule tries to hand a run to Lambda for an hour, 10 times at most.
A run that throws is run again by Lambda twice, a minute and then two
apart. A run that still fails, or that the schedule could not hand over,
waits 14 days in the dead-letter queue, where an alarm tells you;
`docs/runbook.md` says how to run it again. To run the job by hand, invoke
the function (the stack's `FunctionName`) with the same event.

## Develop

Open the repository in its devcontainer, which has Node 24 and the Go toolchain.

```sh
npm ci           # install the exact packages in package-lock.json
npm test         # unit tests and the stacks' assertions, with vitest
npm run synth    # write the development CloudFormation templates into cdk.out/
{{- if .Standard }}
make check       # what CI runs: format, lint, type check, test, synth, cfn-lint
make help        # every target
{{- end }}
```

Nothing here deploys or needs an AWS account: synth runs offline, bundles the
function with the installed esbuild, and needs neither Docker nor credentials.

## Tiers and operations

The stacks are built for a tier, `production` or `development`, which is the
place they run, not the code: `make synth` writes both, into
`cdk.out/production/` and `cdk.out/development/`, and a deploy picks one
(`cdk deploy -c tier=production --all`; without `-c tier` it is `development`).
The production stack is `{{ .Container }}`, the development one
`{{ .Container }}-development`, so a deploy that names no tier never changes
production. Both run on the schedule.
Production keeps the tables and the dead-letter queue when the stack goes,
protects the tables and the stack from deletion, recovers them to any second
of the last 35 days, backs them up daily with AWS Backup, traces with X-Ray
and keeps logs a year; development throws its data away with the stack and
keeps logs a month. Both tiers alarm and have a dashboard.

What the app promises whatever the tier, the backups' retention and copy
region, the recovery objectives and who the alarms tell, is in
`infra/operations.ts`, which C4 Factory renders from the Container's
`spec.operations`: change the block, and the file follows it. The alert email is the
stack's `AlertEmail` parameter, which a deploy can set per environment. Each
stack outputs `EntryPoint`, the schedule's ARN, `FunctionName`, the function
it runs, and `AlarmTopicArn`, the topic its alarms notify.

Deploying (`cdk deploy`, after `cdk bootstrap` of the account) is for its
owner, with their own credentials.

## Layout

- `src/handler.ts` is the Lambda entry point: it creates the DynamoDB client
  and Powertools' Logger and Metrics once, at module scope, reads
  `TABLE_NAME` and `IDEMPOTENCY_TABLE_NAME`, and runs `runJob` once per
  scheduled time through Powertools' Idempotency, after checking the event.
- `src/job.ts` checks the event and does the run's work; `src/runs.ts` holds
  the rules, free of AWS; `src/run_store.ts` keeps runs in DynamoDB behind
  the `RunStore` interface tests fake.
- `infra/stacks.ts` defines the stacks for a tier; `infra/job_stack.ts` is
  the main one: the schedule, its group and role, the function with its log
  group and role, the dead-letter queue, the tables, the alarms
  (`infra/alarms.ts`) and, in production, the backups (`infra/backups.ts`).
  The stack is named after the package. `infra/operations.ts` holds what
  the Container promises.
- `infra/connectors.ts` lists the data stores the Container's Relationships
  connect it to, which C4 Factory renders from their Connectors; the stack
  connects each to the function, alarms on its table and backs it up.
{{- if has .Connectors "dynamodb" }} The
  `dynamodb` DataStore's table is made by `infra/connector_dynamodb.ts`, and
  the function reads its name in `DYNAMODB_TABLE_NAME`. New data goes in that
  table: the `Runs` table (`TABLE_NAME`) is the starter's example.
{{- end }}
{{- if .Standard }}
- `infra/main.ts` is the CDK app `cdk synth` runs (`cdk.json`), and
  `infra/standard.ts` says what each tier means, tags every stack and adds
  CDK Nag's checks. `docs/runbook.md` says how to restore the data, roll a
  deployment back, and what to do when an alarm fires.

Read `AGENTS.md` before changing anything: it is the rulebook, for people and agents.
{{- end }}
